    <section id="inference" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>01 · one model turn</span>
        <h2>The model answers what Dekopon sends now.</h2>
        <p>Model inference is the step that produces the next assistant turn. The output can be final text or a request to run Dekopon’s sandboxed scripting tool.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="request-loop" role="group" aria-label="A bounded model and tool loop">
        <div><span>1 · build</span><strong>Instructions, history, new message, tools</strong><small>Dekopon creates an ordered <code>Vec&lt;ModelMessage&gt;</code>.</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div><span>2 · infer</span><strong>Ask the selected model</strong><small>ChatGPT subscription or an OpenAI-compatible endpoint.</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div><span>3 · decide</span><strong>Answer or tool call</strong><small>A final answer ends the loop.</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↘</i>
        <div class="request-loop-tool"><span>4 · if needed</span><strong>Run one bounded script</strong><small>Append the tool call and result, then ask again.</small></div>
      </div>

      <p>The prompt loop caps model turns and capability calls. Within one session, each new tool call and result is appended to the messages already sent.</p>

      <div class="article-callout callout-plain">
        <strong>Context is explicit</strong>
        <p>Dekopon’s ChatGPT transport sends <code>store: false</code> and the full current <code>input</code>. It does not use <code>previous_response_id</code> or OpenAI Conversations.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="request-growth" class="article-section article-section-wide">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02 · three Slack turns</span>
        <h2>Each follow-up extends the useful prefix.</h2>
        <p>A persistent route keeps final user-and-answer pairs. The diagram shows conceptual model context, not a literal provider token dump.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="growth-legend" role="group" aria-label="Request growth legend">
        <span class="legend-stable"><i aria-hidden="true"></i> stable configuration</span>
        <span class="legend-history"><i aria-hidden="true"></i> remembered exchange</span>
        <span class="legend-new"><i aria-hidden="true"></i> newest message</span>
      </div>

      <div class="turn-growth">
        <article class="turn-card">
          <div class="turn-card-heading"><span>Slack turn 1</span><strong>Start</strong></div>
          <p>“Summarize PR #42.”</p>
          <div class="turn-context" role="group" aria-label="First request context">
            <span class="segment-stable">instructions + tools</span>
            <span class="segment-new">user 1</span>
          </div>
          <small>After the answer: store <code>(user 1, answer 1)</code>.</small>
        </article>
        <article class="turn-card">
          <div class="turn-card-heading"><span>Slack turn 2</span><strong>Continue</strong></div>
          <p>“What could break?”</p>
          <div class="turn-context" role="group" aria-label="Second request context">
            <span class="segment-stable">instructions + tools</span>
            <span class="segment-history">user 1</span>
            <span class="segment-history">answer 1</span>
            <span class="segment-new">user 2</span>
          </div>
          <small>Append the second final exchange, then trim whole oldest turns if needed.</small>
        </article>
        <article class="turn-card">
          <div class="turn-card-heading"><span>Slack turn 3</span><strong>Extend</strong></div>
          <p>“Draft tests for that.”</p>
          <div class="turn-context" role="group" aria-label="Third request context">
            <span class="segment-stable">instructions + tools</span>
            <span class="segment-history">user 1</span>
            <span class="segment-history">answer 1</span>
            <span class="segment-history">user 2</span>
            <span class="segment-history">answer 2</span>
            <span class="segment-new">user 3</span>
          </div>
          <small>The model can resolve “that” because Dekopon supplied the earlier exchange.</small>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="article-callout callout-decision">
        <strong>What gets compacted</strong>
        <p>Between Slack messages, Dekopon remembers the user text and final answer. It drops model reasoning, scripts, tool calls, and tool output.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="conversation-memory" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>03 · Slack conversation memory</span>
        <h2>Choose independent messages or bounded continuity.</h2>
        <p>The route controls the behavior. Memory changes prompt context, not permission.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="conversation-mode-grid">
        <article>
          <span>Default · <code>oneShot</code></span>
          <h3>Every message starts empty</h3>
          <p>Only standing instructions and the new message enter the session. Nothing is remembered for the next Slack message.</p>
          <ul><li>independent prompt sessions</li><li>one route-level cache key</li><li>best for short-lived tasks</li></ul>
        </article>
        <article class="conversation-mode-persistent">
          <span>Opt in · <code>persistent</code></span>
          <h3>Recent exchanges return</h3>
          <p>History is keyed by transport, conversation identity, and sender. Two people in one channel do not share remembered answers.</p>
          <ul><li>question-and-final-answer pairs</li><li>oldest whole turns drop first</li><li>one opaque key per live conversation</li></ul>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="memory-bounds" role="group" aria-label="Default persistent conversation bounds">
        <div><strong>15 min</strong><span>idle timeout</span></div>
        <div><strong>12</strong><span>replayed exchanges</span></div>
        <div><strong>64 KiB</strong><span>replayed text</span></div>
        <div><strong>1,024</strong><span>process-wide conversations</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>These are the gateway defaults after a route chooses <code>persistent</code>. Operators can set the timeout and window. The process-wide count evicts the least recently used entry.</p>
      <p>History lives only in <code>dekopond</code> memory. It is not written to disk or sent to the broker, and a restart forgets it. Idle eviction is lazy: expired text may remain resident until lookup, capacity pressure, or exit, but it is not replayed.</p>
      <p>“In memory” describes durability, not isolation. In the current single-UID deployment, other processes under the owner’s user can read that memory. Operating-system paging and core dumps are outside the daemon’s control.</p>

      <div class="fresh-check-flow" role="group" aria-label="Fresh authorization on each Slack message">
        <div><span>new Slack message</span><strong>1</strong></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div><span>fresh attested broker leg</span><strong>2</strong></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div><span>fresh capability result</span><strong>3</strong></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div><span>then load history</span><strong>4</strong></div>
      </div>
      <p>A stored grant is only an invalidation check. If the broker reports different capability identifiers, Dekopon drops the history and rotates its cache key. An empty result removes the conversation and makes no model call.</p>
      <p>A persistent key lives exactly as long as its conversation entry. It rotates after idle, capacity, a changed grant set, or process restart. A <code>oneShot</code> route gets one key at startup and rotates it on restart. Keys are minted from entropy, carry no sender identity, and grant nothing.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="prompt-caching" class="article-section article-section-wide">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04 · prompt caching</span>
        <h2>Dekopon shapes requests. The provider owns the cache.</h2>
        <p>Prompt caching can avoid recomputing an exact prefix. It is an inference optimization, not memory, storage, isolation, or authorization.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="cache-ownership-grid">
        <article>
          <span>Dekopon implements</span>
          <h3>A cache-friendly request shape</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>stable instructions and tool definitions</li>
            <li>append-ordered conversation messages</li>
            <li>an opaque <code>prompt_cache_key</code> hint</li>
            <li>provider-reported cached token accounting</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="provider-owned">
          <span>The model provider controls</span>
          <h3>Whether reuse happens</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>prefix matching and model eligibility</li>
            <li>routing, placement, and eviction</li>
            <li>retention and supported options</li>
            <li>reported cached tokens and pricing</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h3 class="subsection-title">What OpenAI documents for its public API</h3>
      <div class="public-cache-facts">
        <article><span>Exact</span><strong>Shared prefix required</strong><p>A change ends reuse at the first differing token. Put stable content before changing content.</p></article>
        <article><span>1,024</span><strong>Input-token minimum</strong><p>Shorter prompts do not receive cached-token credit. Characters and bytes cannot prove eligibility.</p></article>
        <article><span>5–10 min</span><strong>Typical idle retention</strong><p>The default in-memory cache generally lasts minutes after inactivity and can sometimes last up to one hour.</p></article>
        <article><span>24h</span><strong>Explicit extended option</strong><p><code>prompt_cache_retention</code> supports 24 hours only on models where OpenAI explicitly offers it.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p>On public Responses, cached input appears at <code>usage.input_tokens_details.cached_tokens</code>. Cached tokens still count as input and use the model’s documented cached-input price.</p>

      <div class="article-callout callout-warning private-endpoint-warning">
        <strong>The subscription caveat is load-bearing</strong>
        <p>Dekopon uses <code>https://chatgpt.com/backend-api/codex/responses</code> for ChatGPT-plan access. OpenAI does not publicly promise the Platform API’s cache behavior, retention, parameters, accounting, or pricing for this private endpoint. Its cache behavior is unknown.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="cache-lifetime" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>05 · process lifetime ≠ cache lifetime</span>
        <h2>A long-lived local agent pins no provider cache.</h2>
        <p>The documented cache lives on provider infrastructure. Client objects and local processes do not hold a lease on it.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="cache-lifetime-board">
        <div class="lifetime-local">
          <span>Your hardware</span>
          <strong><code>dekopond</code> stays alive</strong>
          <small>history + opaque routing key</small>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">sends matching requests →</i>
        <div class="lifetime-provider">
          <span>Provider infrastructure</span>
          <strong>cache may be warm or cold</strong>
          <small>provider decides placement and eviction</small>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ul class="not-a-lease-list">
        <li><strong>Same process:</strong> keeping <code>dekopond</code> running preserves local history, not provider cache retention.</li>
        <li><strong>Same client or connection:</strong> Dekopon currently rebuilds its model client per gateway message. Reusing one would still not create a documented cache lease.</li>
        <li><strong>Same login:</strong> an OAuth token or ChatGPT account authenticates a request. It does not document cache lifetime.</li>
        <li><strong>Same cache key:</strong> the key helps route likely shared prefixes. It does not create, refresh, delete, isolate, or guarantee a cache entry.</li>
        <li><strong>Response state:</strong> public API response IDs and Conversations carry context. They are separate from prompt-cache retention, and Dekopon does not use them here.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>A persistent conversation can outlive a warm provider cache. The next request still works because Dekopon sends the context again; it may simply pay full inference cost.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="rust-shape" class="article-section article-section-wide">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>06 · important Rust seams</span>
        <h2>The types keep context, routing, and permission separate.</h2>
        <p>The canonical document contains the full literal walkthrough. This is the compact map to the code.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="inference-type-grid">
        <article><span>model.rs</span><h3><code>ModelMessage</code></h3><p>One system, user, assistant, or tool message in ordered context.</p></article>
        <article><span>prompt/history.rs</span><h3><code>History</code></h3><p>A bounded list of compact <code>ConversationTurn</code> values.</p></article>
        <article><span>conversation.rs</span><h3><code>ConversationStore</code></h3><p>The in-memory per-sender window, grant snapshot, cache key, and idle clock.</p></article>
        <article><span>model.rs</span><h3><code>CompletionOptions</code></h3><p>Request-scoped routing metadata. It changes no model content or authority.</p></article>
      </div>

      <div class="almanac-code-block inference-code-block">
        <div class="code-block-label"><span>compact pre-wire shape</span><a href="https://github.com/dekopon-agents/dekopon/blob/main/docs/inference.md">full literal walkthrough ↗</a></div>
        <pre><code><span class="code-comment">// abridged across dekopond/session.rs and dekopon-agent/prompt.rs</span>
<span class="code-purple">let mut</span> messages = Vec::new();
<span class="code-purple">if let</span> Some(system) = system {
    messages.push(ModelMessage::system(system));
}
history.replay_into(<span class="code-purple">&amp;mut</span> messages);
messages.push(ModelMessage::user(prompt));

<span class="code-purple">let</span> options = CompletionOptions::default()
    .with_prompt_cache_key(cache_key);
model.complete_with(<span class="code-purple">&amp;</span>messages, <span class="code-purple">&amp;</span>tools, <span class="code-purple">&amp;</span>options)?;</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="wire-shape-grid">
        <article><span>ChatGPT/Codex Responses shape</span><code>instructions</code><code>input[]</code><code>tools[]</code><code>store: false</code><code>prompt_cache_key?</code></article>
        <article><span>OpenAI-compatible chat shape</span><code>messages[]</code><code>tools[]</code><code>tool_choice: auto</code><code>prompt_cache_key?</code></article>
      </div>
      <p>The key is omitted when blank. A backend that ignores routing metadata receives the same conversation and can return the same answer without a cache discount.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="future-memory" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>07 · beyond one conversation</span>
        <h2>Conversation history is not long-term agent memory.</h2>
        <p>Today, the agent remembers nothing once its bounded conversation is evicted or <code>dekopond</code> restarts.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="future-memory-grid">
        <article>
          <span>Current</span>
          <h3>Recent conversational continuity</h3>
          <p>Replay final exchanges so follow-ups make sense. Keep the window small, per sender, and in process.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Possible later framework</span>
          <h3>Selected, durable knowledge</h3>
          <p>Summarize or index useful facts, retrieve only relevant context, preserve provenance, and support explicit redaction and deletion.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p>A future memory system could span conversations, survive restarts, and reduce repeated context. It would also create a new sensitive data store and a longer prompt-injection dwell time. Dekopon does not claim one today.</p>

